                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1439-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>Sterling Audio ST66 Tube</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Tube Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The ST66 is a side-address vacuum tube condenser based on the Groove-Tubes GT66.</p><p>The mic has fixed-cardioid polar pattern, switchable 75Hz low rolloff, and switchable -10dB pad. A low noise, low heat Groove Tubes 6205 sub-miniature tube is used.</p><p>The mic has very high sensitivity, at 35mV/Pa. This is a characteristic of most Groove Tubes and Sterling mics.</p><p>The one-inch brass capsule features 3-micron gold-evaporated Mylar diaphragm and unique <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/tag/disk-resonator">Disk Resonator</a> for improved high-frequency response. As with all Groove Tubes and Sterling large-condenser capsules, it is &#8220;oven baked&#8221; to remove residual moisture, helping ensure consistent performance.</p><p>The mic ships with: a zippered storage pouch, the SM4 shockmount, a 7-pin XLR cable, and the mic&#8217;s power supply (p/n STPSM1).<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Sterling/ST66">Sterling Audio ST66 Tube</a> is also known as: ST-66.</p>
